WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.805
Parallel circuits feel different from series circuits because the structure is different from the start.

2
00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:09.610
Instead of one path, the current has branch choices.

3
00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:14.415
That means the current can split between branches and recombine later.

4
00:00:14.415 --> 00:00:19.220
But because each branch is connected across the same two supply points, the potential difference across every branch is the same as the supply.

5
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.025
Once those two ideas are secure, the rest of the lesson becomes much easier to predict.

6
00:00:24.025 --> 00:00:28.830
Keep this takeaway in view: Parallel reasoning begins with branch structure, then applies the voltage and current rules.

7
00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:33.635
Structure means multiple branches.

8
00:00:33.635 --> 00:00:38.440
Charges have more than one path to travel through.

9
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.245
Voltage means same on each branch.

10
00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:48.050
Every branch sits across the same supply points.

11
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:54.768
In a parallel diagram, each branch connects across the same two points of the supply.

12
00:00:54.768 --> 00:01:01.485
That is why the potential difference across every branch is the same.

13
00:01:01.485 --> 00:01:08.202
But the current does not have to be the same in each branch, because the charges can divide between the available routes depending on the resistance of each branch.

14
00:01:08.202 --> 00:01:14.920
When the branches join back together, the total current is the sum of the branch currents.

15
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:21.637
The structure itself explains the rules.

16
00:01:21.637 --> 00:01:28.355
Keep this takeaway in view: The branch layout explains both the same-voltage rule and the split-current rule.

17
00:01:28.355 --> 00:01:35.072
Same two points: Every branch is connected across the same supply terminals.

18
00:01:35.072 --> 00:01:41.790
Same branch voltage: That is why each branch has the full supply voltage.

19
00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:46.653
Imagine a road that splits into two routes and then rejoins at the same destination.

20
00:01:46.653 --> 00:01:51.515
The cars can divide between the two routes, so each route may carry a different flow.

21
00:01:51.515 --> 00:01:56.378
But both routes begin and end at the same places.

22
00:01:56.378 --> 00:02:01.241
That is similar to a parallel circuit.

23
00:02:01.241 --> 00:02:06.104
Each branch connects across the same two points, so the branch voltage is common.

24
00:02:06.104 --> 00:02:10.966
The traffic analogy helps because it makes the idea of splitting and recombining much more natural.

25
00:02:10.966 --> 00:02:15.829
Keep this takeaway in view: Parallel circuits are branch-route stories before they are equation stories.

26
00:02:15.829 --> 00:02:20.692
Two routes means parallel branches.

27
00:02:20.692 --> 00:02:25.555
The flow can divide between the available paths.

28
00:02:25.555 --> 00:02:30.417
Same endpoints means same voltage.

29
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:35.280
Each branch connects across the same two circuit points.

30
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.227
Two relations matter most here.

31
00:02:40.227 --> 00:02:45.175
First, the total current equals the sum of the branch currents.

32
00:02:45.175 --> 00:02:50.122
Second, the voltage across each branch equals the supply voltage.

33
00:02:50.122 --> 00:02:55.069
Those rules fit together because the circuit splits into branches that share the same endpoints.

34
00:02:55.069 --> 00:03:00.016
If another branch is added, the total current can increase because the circuit now offers another route for charge flow.

35
00:03:00.016 --> 00:03:04.964
Keep this takeaway in view: Parallel formulas are just compact versions of the branch logic.

36
00:03:04.964 --> 00:03:09.911
Current rule: Total current is the sum of the currents in the branches.

37
00:03:09.911 --> 00:03:14.858
Voltage rule: Each branch has the same potential difference as the supply.

38
00:03:14.858 --> 00:03:19.805
I_total = I1 + I2 + ...

39
00:03:19.805 --> 00:03:24.753
V_branch = V_supply.

40
00:03:24.753 --> 00:03:29.700
Each branch gets the full supply voltage, while the current divides between the branches.

41
00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:35.344
When another branch is added in parallel, the circuit offers an extra path for charge flow.

42
00:03:35.344 --> 00:03:40.989
That means the total resistance of the whole circuit becomes smaller, and for the same supply voltage the total current can become larger.

43
00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:46.633
This is different from series circuits, where adding a resistor makes the total resistance larger.

44
00:03:46.633 --> 00:03:52.278
Parallel circuits reward branch-based thinking: more routes usually mean easier overall flow.

45
00:03:52.278 --> 00:03:57.922
Keep this takeaway in view: More parallel branches usually mean easier total flow through the circuit.

46
00:03:57.922 --> 00:04:03.567
Add branch means more paths.

47
00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:09.211
Charge has an extra route through the circuit.

48
00:04:09.211 --> 00:04:14.856
Whole-circuit effect means lower total resistance.

49
00:04:14.856 --> 00:04:20.500
The circuit becomes easier overall for charge to move through.

50
00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:25.865
A common mistake is to assume that same voltage must mean same current in every branch.

51
00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:31.230
That is only true when the branch resistances are the same.

52
00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:36.595
In general, current depends on both the voltage and the resistance of the branch.

53
00:04:36.595 --> 00:04:41.960
So the same supply voltage across two different branches can still produce different branch currents.

54
00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:47.325
Keeping that distinction clear helps learners avoid overgeneralizing the same-voltage rule.

55
00:04:47.325 --> 00:04:52.690
Keep this takeaway in view: Same branch voltage does not automatically mean same branch current.

56
00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:58.055
Parallel branches share the same voltage.

57
00:04:58.055 --> 00:05:03.420
Branch currents do not have to be equal.

58
00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:07.097
Ask yourself three quick questions.

59
00:05:07.097 --> 00:05:10.774
In a parallel circuit, what is the same across each branch?

60
00:05:10.774 --> 00:05:14.451
The voltage.

61
00:05:14.451 --> 00:05:18.128
What happens to the current at a split?

62
00:05:18.128 --> 00:05:21.805
It divides between the branches.

63
00:05:21.805 --> 00:05:25.482
And how do you find the total current after the branches rejoin?

64
00:05:25.482 --> 00:05:29.158
Add the branch currents.

65
00:05:29.158 --> 00:05:32.835
Those three statements are the lesson core.

66
00:05:32.835 --> 00:05:36.512
Keep this takeaway in view: Parallel means same voltage, split current, and added total current.

67
00:05:36.512 --> 00:05:40.189
Same quantity means voltage.

68
00:05:40.189 --> 00:05:43.866
Each branch gets the full supply potential difference.

69
00:05:43.866 --> 00:05:47.543
At the split means current divides.

70
00:05:47.543 --> 00:05:51.220
The flow can separate into the available routes.

71
00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:56.674
You are ready for the worked example now.

72
00:05:56.674 --> 00:06:02.127
In a parallel circuit, each branch gets the full supply voltage, and the total current is found by adding the branch currents.

73
00:06:02.127 --> 00:06:07.581
So if one branch carries 2 amperes and another carries 3 amperes, the total current is 5 amperes.

74
00:06:07.581 --> 00:06:13.035
Keep the meaning attached: the current split across the branches and then recombined.

75
00:06:13.035 --> 00:06:18.488
The same-voltage rule and the added-current rule come directly from the branch structure.

76
00:06:18.488 --> 00:06:23.942
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: use the branch rules, then calculate the total current.

77
00:06:23.942 --> 00:06:29.395
Step 1: Recognize the circuit as parallel because it has branches.

78
00:06:29.395 --> 00:06:34.849
Step 2: Read or calculate the current in each branch.

79
00:06:34.849 --> 00:06:40.303
I_total = I1 + I2.

80
00:06:40.303 --> 00:06:45.756
I_total = 2 + 3.

81
00:06:45.756 --> 00:06:51.210
Use the branch-current rule because this is a parallel circuit.
